Results | From Task Bloat to a 90-Day Strategy
The question behind the project
One of the hardest questions in Account Management is: How will you prioritize?

I wanted to see whether Python could help answer that question for a newly assigned Ad-Tech Account Manager inheriting 50 advertiser accounts representing $215,850 in monthly advertising budgets. The challenge was not simply to identify the busiest accounts. It was to build a credible 90-day plan that balanced client relationships, campaign health, retention, and growth within a 40-hour workweek.

Unlike a conventional data-in/data-out model, this required accounting for competing responsibilities and decisions that depend on human judgment.
How the model evolved
Iteration	What changed	What I learned
V1	Built a detailed, task-by-task schedule.	The model filled all 520 available hours but still left 77.5 hours of work backlogged. A full calendar was not the same as a workable strategy.
V2	Reduced the backlog and tightened scheduling.	The backlog fell to 0.5 hours, but key weekly goals still went missing, including introductions and growth activities. Optimizing task volume did not solve prioritization.
V3	Rebuilt the output as a 13-week strategic roadmap.	Weekly objectives became clearer, but the model sometimes selected troubled accounts for growth-focused weeks.
V4	Introduced phase-specific scoring, growth eligibility checks, and a portfolio snapshot.	Business logic improved, but one account appeared in 11 of 13 weekly spotlight lists. High risk could dominate the entire plan.
V5	Added account history and rotation.	Rotation reduced repetition, but occasionally promoted very small accounts over more material business priorities. Fairness was not the goal.
V6	Put phase fit and commercial importance ahead of rotation; maintained continuity between growth discovery and follow-up.	The model began to reflect how an AM would actually balance urgent work with commercial opportunity.
V7	Clarified tier-based service response expectations and corrected the spotlight audit.	Routine service needed to remain separate from strategic focus, and the audit needed to count only accounts actually selected.
V8	Added one executive-level priority for each of the three planning phases.	The roadmap could now communicate a management recommendation, not just 13 weeks of detail.
Final version	Replaced approximate day-range labels with Month 1, Month 2, and Month 3 planning phases.	The final presentation became easier to read without changing the underlying selection logic.


The biggest shift: from tasks to decisions
The earliest versions tried to answer, “What should the AM do every hour?” That created task bloat: too many activities, competing deadlines, and a plan that looked precise without being especially useful.

The final version asks a better question: “What deserves the AM's strategic attention this week, and why?”

Each week now includes a primary objective, a deliverable to verify, five account spotlights with supporting reasons, a 40-hour allocation, and a practical trade-off. The spotlights are additional strategic attention, not the only accounts receiving service.

The model reserves 26 hours for strategic priorities, 10 for recurring operations, and 4 for contingency each week. Those are planning allocations—not claims that work was completed.
Final result: three recommendations that tell the story
Month 1 — Establish control of the book (Weeks 1–4)
Major recommendation: Complete the 50-account baseline audit and establish ownership, contact coverage, and risk-ranked account plans before deep optimization.

The AM first needs to understand what they have inherited. That means identifying budget exposure, open issues, campaign exceptions, decision makers, and upcoming reviews before committing too much time to individual fixes.
Month 2 — Protect retention and delivery (Weeks 5–8)
Major recommendation: Concentrate strategic capacity on material campaign issues and upcoming reviews; verify progress and re-rank using live evidence.

The focus shifts from understanding risks to addressing them. A healthy portfolio is a stronger foundation for future commercial conversations than an indiscriminate push for expansion.
Month 3 — Qualify growth and set the next-quarter plan (Weeks 9–13)
Major recommendation: Advance only evidence-qualified expansion opportunities and use observed spend and open risks to build a credible next-quarter plan.

Growth opportunities must be supported by campaign performance and client goals. Week 9 discovery candidates can continue into Week 10 commercial discussions only if actual evidence supports that next step.
