Why only 25 of 50 accounts in the spotlight audit? Because equal strategic attention is not necessarily effective prioritization. Every account still requires appropriate recurring coverage, while additional strategic attention should follow risk, commercial exposure, milestone urgency, and phase relevance.
What this result does—and does not—prove
The project demonstrates a repeatable decision framework, not a simulation of client outcomes. The data is synthetic and the baseline remains frozen. The script does not know whether a client replied, a campaign recovered, a renewal closed, or an expansion opportunity converted.

In practice, an AM would re-check CRM records, campaign performance, client feedback, and open issues each week before acting on the recommendations. The script also distinguishes advertiser budgets from actual billed spend and platform revenue.
Takeaway
The biggest improvement was not making the algorithm more complicated. It was changing what I asked it to produce.

I started with a crowded task list and ended with a strategy that a manager could explain: understand the book, protect the business, then pursue qualified growth.

That feels closer to the real work of Account Management. Python can help organize competing signals and make trade-offs visible, but the final decision still requires human judgment.
